 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Upper threshold",
   "expr": "VHI = (V+ - 1 - VREF) × R1 / (R1 + R2) + VREF",
   "where": {
    "V+": "positive supply voltage",
    "VREF": "reference at the far end of R1, the centre of the window",
    "R1": "resistor from VREF to +IN",
    "R2": "feedback resistor from OUT to +IN"
   },
   "note": "Input level above which the output changes state.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Lower threshold",
   "expr": "VLO = VREF × (1 - R1 / (R1 + R2))",
   "where": {
    "VREF": "reference voltage",
    "R1": "resistor from VREF to +IN",
    "R2": "feedback resistor from OUT to +IN"
   },
   "note": "Input level below which the output changes back.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Feedback pole",
   "expr": "fP = 1 / (2π × CF × R2)",
   "where": {
    "CF": "capacitor across R2",
    "R2": "feedback resistor"
   },
   "note": "Above fP the thresholds approach VHI = V+ - 1 V and VLO = 0 V; below it they keep the values of the two equations above.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Input time constant",
   "expr": "τ = RS × CIN",
   "where": {
    "RS": "source resistance at an input",
    "CIN": "input plus stray capacitance at that pin"
   },
   "note": "With RS = 3 kΩ and 5 pF, τ = 15 ns, against a comparator response of about 5 ns.",
   "page": 7
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Architecture",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "One comparator, with its input stage and output stage on separate supply pins. The input section runs from ±5 V or from a single +5 V; the outputs are a complementary pair, OUT and /OUT. Rising and falling propagation delays are close to equal and move together with temperature, so the output duty cycle follows the input duty cycle. Pinout matches the LT1016."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input stage",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A PNP differential pair lets the common-mode range reach the negative rail and run up to 2.2 V below V+. Common-mode voltage is the mean of the two input voltages; the response is fastest with it inside this range, and on a +5 V supply inputs above 3.0 V slow it. Bias current, about 3 µA, flows out of the pins; it falls to zero on the input that is high and doubles on the one that is low, so large input resistors produce noticeable voltage drops."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitance and source resistance",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Input capacitance is about 3 pF, found by adding 5 kΩ in series with an input and measuring the change in delay. With strays the total at a pin can be several picofarads, and together with source resistance it slows the edge reaching the input. Source impedance under 1 kΩ gives the best speed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bypassing and ground",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Put a 1 µF electrolytic from each supply pin (pin 1 and pin 4) to ground, within 0.5 inch of the pin, plus a 10 nF ceramic as close to the supply pins as possible. A continuous ground plane, broken only where current paths require it, keeps ground inductance low and limits ground bounce and stray capacitance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Latch",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "With LATCH high the outputs hold their state whatever the inputs do; with LATCH low the comparator tracks its inputs again, and new data reaches the output about 8 ns after LATCH falls. For a reliable hold, the input must be settled at least 5 ns before LATCH rises (setup 2 ns to 3 ns) and stay settled 3 ns after (hold). Logic high is 2.0 V minimum and logic low 0.8 V maximum, compatible with TTL and CMOS; the latch input has no hysteresis."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output loading",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The output can supply up to 40 mA with little change in delay. Load limits: no more than twenty TTL inputs and no resistance below 100 Ω. Capacitive load above 50 pF causes ringing and lowers bandwidth."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output swing",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Output swing runs from 0.2 V above ground to 1.1 V below V+. A 5 kΩ pull-up from the output to V+ extends the high level, useful on low supply voltages, and leaves the specifications unchanged; it is not needed in normal use."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Replacing an LT1016",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Same pins, with five differences. Input range on +5 V: 0 V to 3.0 V, against 1.25 V to 3.5 V for the LT1016; inputs above 3.0 V slow the response, inputs well above it also draw more input current, and if both exceed it they can be shifted or attenuated into range, while one input slightly above 3.0 V with the other well inside may work unchanged. Bias current: -3 µA typical flowing out, against +5 µA flowing in. Speed: 6.75 ns typical against 10 ns, so system timing may need revisiting. Swing: slightly wider. Supply current: about 5 mA against 25 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Hysteresis",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Positive feedback from the output to +IN through R2, with R1 to a reference, gives the comparator a switching window so a noisy input near the threshold does not make the output chatter. The window is centred on VREF and its width set by R1 / (R1 + R2)."
  }
 ],
